{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787596085,"nonce":"UTtUfrth0TQOp494Rx+veZLL65s=","title":"Found while fixing 8de85a8 (the dogfood `version` step that failed with class Step and","message":"no Pod — root cause: a bash COMMENT spelling ${{ outputs.version.tag }}, unresolvable at\nlaunch because the interpolation context is built from `needs` only).\n\nThe gap: for plain steps there is NO compile-time check that ${{ outputs.X.* }} names a\nstep in the consumer's `needs`. Only invoke references get that validation (scarab-pipeline\nhas \"reading an output without needing the invoke is a compile error\" — the same rule does\nnot exist for step-result references). So a typo'd or out-of-needs reference — or one\nhiding in a comment, since interpolation is textual over the whole command string — is\nonly discovered at LAUNCH, as a per-attempt fail-fast (now FailureKind::Config since\n8e11b01), instead of at pipeline compile.\n\nFix direction: in scarab-pipeline validation, resolve every ${{ outputs.X }} occurrence in\nstep commands against the step's transitive `needs`; unknown X = compile error, same shape\nas the invoke rule. Decide deliberately whether to keep textual scanning (comments trigger\nit — surprising but consistent) or to document escaping; either way the compile error\nmakes the launch-time trap unreachable for well-formed pipelines.","files":null}]}